WEBVTT
Kind: subtitles
Language: en

1
00:00.360 --> 00:04.200
So what does using the Problem Statement Worksheet
in the moment actually look like?

2
00:04.200 --> 00:10.200
Well, in the moment, you're unlikely to actually — without a sheet of paper —
go through all the different components and fill it out.

3
00:10.200 --> 00:15.520
But what you can do is think about what the different components
of that Problem Statement Worksheet are,

4
00:15.720 --> 00:18.820
and what questions you could ask.
Let me give you a real example from this morning.

5
00:19.370 --> 00:23.550
I woke up this morning and there was a water main break
outside my apartment on the Upper West Side.

6
00:23.940 --> 00:29.090
And there was a problem: the street was flooded.
And so I needed to think through, in the moment,

7
00:29.670 --> 00:34.180
how was I going to deal with it — and think through
what the problem is that I need to solve.

8
00:34.500 --> 00:38.400
Obviously, there's a water main break, but importantly,
who are all the stakeholders that are going to be impacted?

9
00:38.400 --> 00:41.470
How am I going to get to the office to do this video shoot?

10
00:41.930 --> 00:47.170
How are my children going to get to school this morning?
How are the deliveries that were supposed to come that afternoon

11
00:47.490 --> 00:51.000
going to be received or impacted by this water main break?

12
00:51.000 --> 00:57.400
And so going through that structured approach enabled me to think through,
very rapidly in that moment, how best to address this problem.

13
00:57.400 --> 01:03.000
When you're filling out the Problem Statement Worksheet in the moment,
there are a couple of mistakes that we commonly see.

14
01:03.430 --> 01:07.200
One is to think about the stakeholders — and the key here

15
01:07.200 --> 01:13.400
is to really ensure you've thought about all the relevant stakeholders that need to be
involved in order to actually have your recommendation be actionable.

16
01:13.400 --> 01:17.470
Another is the scope of the solution set. Have you been very clear,

17
01:17.540 --> 01:21.120
and is everybody aligned on what is in scope versus what is out of scope?

18
01:21.510 --> 01:26.400
And the third is thinking through: what are the constraints?
And here it's being really honest

19
01:26.400 --> 01:31.000
in terms of what the constraints are that one needs to solve for.

20
01:31.000 --> 01:37.120
And that has implications, then, for thinking through the time and the resources
you have available, and how best to prioritise your work moving forward.

21
01:37.290 --> 01:43.970
The Problem Statement Worksheet is one of the most effective tools, I think,
that we can actually use as problem solvers.

22
01:44.420 --> 01:49.400
And the way that I always thought about it when I first started in my career
was, I thought: “Oh my gosh,

23
01:49.400 --> 01:53.600
I have to have an answer for every single box on this robust worksheet.”

24
01:53.600 --> 02:01.280
And the truth of the matter is, I think that sometimes the most powerful way
to use the tool is to use it in a dynamic, ad hoc way.

25
02:01.570 --> 02:05.790
What are the ways that we can ask the right questions to tease out the answer,

26
02:06.120 --> 02:09.150
even if we don't actually have the time to robustly fill it out?

27
02:09.480 --> 02:16.450
The Problem Statement Worksheet should not be something
that you start on day one and never refresh.

28
02:16.810 --> 02:23.400
It should be something that you actually — as you learn more about the problem,
as you understand more stakeholders —

29
02:23.400 --> 02:30.750
you continue to reiterate, so that it becomes something
that's truly the latest and greatest in thinking.
